{
  "$schema": "https://biomejs.dev/schemas/2.5.10/schema.json",
  "vcs": { "enabled": true, "clientKind": "git", "useIgnoreFile": true },

  "files": {
    // src/web/tailwind.css is excluded because Biome's CSS parser does not know
    // Tailwind v4's import modifiers — `layer()` it accepts, `prefix(tw)` and
    // `source(none)` it does not, and it reports 9 PARSE errors rather than
    // lint findings. Parse errors also mean an inline `biome-ignore` in the
    // file cannot help: the suppression is never reached, because the file
    // never parses. Both modifiers are load-bearing (see that file's header),
    // so the exclusion is the only lever. Everything else under src/ is still
    // linted, this one file is not linted at all.
    // This list is an ALLOWLIST, which is the trap in it: a path that is not
    // named here is linted by nothing, and says so nowhere. `api/`, `evals/`,
    // `styles/` and drizzle.config.ts were all in that hole — typechecked by
    // scripts/typecheck.ts, linted by no one — until 2026-08-26. Add the path
    // here as well as to the `lint` script in package.json; the script alone
    // does nothing.
    "includes": [
      "src/**",
      "!src/web/tailwind.css",
      "api/**",
      "tests/**",
      "scripts/**",
      "evals/**",
      // The extraction fixtures are whole saved web pages, and one of them —
      // whatwg.html, the HTML Standard, 772KB — nests about 3,700 elements
      // deep. Biome's HTML parser recurses once per level, and a macOS worker
      // thread gets a 512KB stack, so it blows it: every `biome lint` in this
      // repo died with `thread 'biome::workspace_worker_N' has overflowed its
      // stack` from 2026-08-28, when the fixtures landed, and **exited 0 while
      // doing so** — a linter reporting success having checked nothing
      // (docs/reusable/silent-success.md). Naming a smaller scope does not
      // help: the scanner walks the whole allowlist whatever path you pass it.
      // These are captured inputs, not our code; there is nothing here to lint.
      "!evals/extraction/fixtures/**",
      // Captured model answers, and the same reasoning one line up: not our
      // code, nothing here to lint. `<slug>.raw.json` is *deliberately* the
      // model's reply saved before anything touched it — evals/illustrated/run.ts
      // says so twice — so several of them are a JSON object wrapped in a
      // ```json fence, which is what the model actually sent. Biome parses them
      // because of the extension and reports 16 parse errors, which failed
      // `npm run check`'s `cycles` gate for everybody in the tree from commit
      // 8d8aaadf. Stripping the fences or renaming to .txt would both destroy
      // the one property these files exist for; not linting captured data is
      // the fix.
      "!evals/results/**",
      "styles/**",
      "vite.config.ts",
      "vitest.config.ts",
      "vite.api.config.ts",
      "drizzle.config.ts"
    ]
  },

  // This file is biome.jsonc, not biome.json, and the extension is load-bearing.
  // Biome only parses comments in a .jsonc file. Put a // comment in biome.json
  // and it drops everything after it — no error, no warning, exit code
  // unchanged, and every rule you thought you had configured quietly reverts to
  // its default. See docs/reusable/silent-success.md.

  // Formatter off on purpose. Biome's default is tabs; this codebase is spaces,
  // so enabling it rewrites every file at once — a diff nobody can review, in a
  // tree where several agents have edits in flight. Lint first. Turning the
  // formatter on would be its own commit, if we ever want it at all.
  // Off, and measured rather than assumed: turning it on at its best setting
  // (space/2, lineWidth 100) rewrites 1,239 lines across 39 of 56 files, and
  // the changes are a wash — some collapse a needlessly-wrapped call, others
  // explode a deliberately grouped list (the tag names in src/blocks.ts go from
  // 4 readable lines to 15, one per line). Not worth the churn.
  //
  // useEditorconfig matters even so: if anyone ever flips `enabled` to true,
  // Biome's own default is TABS, and it ignores .editorconfig unless asked.
  // With this on it picks up space/2 from .editorconfig instead of silently
  // reformatting the entire codebase to tabs on the first run.
  "formatter": { "enabled": false, "useEditorconfig": true },

  // Same reason: organizeImports would reorder imports across the whole tree.
  "assist": { "enabled": false },

  "linter": {
    "enabled": true,
    "rules": {
      "recommended": true,

      "nursery": {
        // Catches `doThing()` where doThing is async and nobody awaits it. The
        // failure mode is silence — the work never happens and nothing says so
        // — which is the house bug (docs/reusable/silent-success.md).
        // It lives in `nursery` because it needs type inference; that means it
        // can move or change between Biome versions, which is why
        // @biomejs/biome is pinned to an exact version in package.json.
        "noFloatingPromises": "error"
      },

      // The three rules below are the whole of this repo's "static analysis"
      // that is not Knip — see docs/project/static-analysis.md. All three were
      // already in the installed Biome and simply not switched on, which is why
      // they cost no new dependency.
      "suspicious": {
        // Import cycles. Biome parses with its own parser rather than tsc, so
        // unlike dependency-cruiser and every other graph tool this survives
        // TypeScript 7 removing the compiler API. 183 files in 14ms.
        // Green when switched on (2026-08-26) and *proved red* against a
        // two-file fixture first — a check nobody has watched fail is not yet
        // a check (docs/reusable/silent-success.md).
        "noImportCycles": "error"
      },

      // useImportExtensions is NOT here, and must not be added back without
      // reading this. It looks like an exact fit — this repo writes `./arc.js`
      // meaning `arc.ts`, which is what `forceJsExtensions: true` is for — and
      // it finds zero real problems, because every TS import already complies.
      //
      // What it does find is `import "./tailwind.css"` in src/web/main.tsx,
      // which is correct code. The rule does not understand CSS side-effect
      // imports, reports the line as missing an extension, and marks itself
      // FIXABLE — and the fix it applies is:
      //
      //     - import "./tailwind.css";
      //     + import "./tailwind.js";
      //
      // which points the only stylesheet entry point at a file that does not
      // exist. Anyone running `npm run lint:fix` would have shipped an
      // unstyled app and a green linter. Verified by running it, 2026-08-26.
      //
      // Zero true positives against one silent catastrophe is not a trade, so
      // the rule is off. Revisit if Biome learns to skip non-JS imports.

      "complexity": {
        // Triage, not a gate — hence `info`, and hence 25 rather than the
        // default 15. At 15 this reports 41 functions, which is enough to bury
        // the findings that mean something (the same argument that turned
        // noNonNullAssertion off, below). At 25 it reports the ~16 worth
        // looking at.
        //
        // Read a high score as "go and look", never as "this is wrong": the
        // metric punishes a long flat `switch` about as hard as genuinely
        // nested logic, and the worst score in the repo is a flat route
        // dispatcher that is fine as it is.
        //
        // Counts measured 2026-08-26 and *not* to be trusted later — this tree
        // moves hourly. Re-measure before arguing from them.
        "noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity": {
          "level": "info",
          "options": { "maxAllowedComplexity": 25 }
        }
      },

      "style": {
        // Off deliberately. Every hit is an `x!`, and the count is climbing:
        // tsconfig.base.json turns on noUncheckedIndexedAccess, so each
        // blocks[i] now needs a real check or a `!`. Whether a given `!` is a
        // lie is a question about that line's logic, which this rule cannot
        // see — so it flags all 83 and buries the findings that mean something.
        "noNonNullAssertion": "off"
      }
    }
  },

  "overrides": [
    {
      // noFocusedTests looks for bare calls to `fit`/`fdescribe` — Jasmine's
      // focused-it. tests/layout.test.ts defines a local helper genuinely named
      // `fit` (it wraps fitView from src/web/layout.ts), so the rule fires 19
      // times there and every one is wrong. Scoped off to that one file rather
      // than globally, so a real `it.only` left behind elsewhere still fails.
      "includes": ["tests/layout.test.ts"],
      "linter": { "rules": { "suspicious": { "noFocusedTests": "off" } } }
    }
  ]
}
